{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And she shall weep for her father and for her mother a month of days.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this period of time that Scripture assigned to her contains several benefits. The first is that Scripture imposed upon her the law of mourning, which is thirty days, so that she may fulfill her obligation toward her father and mother if she heard that they were killed while she was still a captive — for a person is obligated to honor father and mother during their lifetime and also after death. The second is that perhaps during this time the desirer's passion will cool and his desire will pass from her when he sees her disheveled with her head shaved and her nails grown and wearing the garments of a captive, for she has already removed the beautiful garments she came in, and upon seeing her weeping and grieving he will send her away to herself. The third is that the memory of idolatry will be uprooted from her mouth — for she had been accustomed to mention it constantly — and it will be forgotten from her heart during this time; and similarly she will despair of her family and her father's house and cleave to this man whom she is destined to be with forever, and then she will convert and immerse and become his wife. And the reason Scripture did not mention the matter of her immersion is that it relied upon what is written elsewhere (Numbers 31:19): אתם ושביכם — for it is well known that she requires the waters of a menstruant." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And she shall weep for her father and for her mother' — this is idolatry, as it is said (Jeremiah 2:27): אומרים לעץ אבי אתה ולאבן את ילדתני. And in Yevamot, chapter HaCholetz: 'Her father and her mother' — Rabbi Akiva says: this is idolatry, and thus it says: אומרים לעץ אבי אתה ולאבן וגו', end of quote. And it appears to me according to this Midrash that the period was 'a month of days' because it encompasses all the circuits: the circuit of the solar day, and the circuit of the seven planets' course corresponding to the seven days of the week, and the circuit of the lunar month. And behold, all of this is included in idolatry, concerning which Israel are warned by the verse (Deuteronomy 4:19): ופן תשא עיניך השמימה וראית את השמש ואת הירח ואת הכוכבים. And 'her father' is an allusion to the sun and 'her mother' to the moon; and the plural form אתין comes to include the stars. And even if she had a father and mother, the Torah commands her to weep and to complete the statute together with her idolatry when she separates from them and from their worship, for the Torah did not warn concerning idolatry except to Israel and not to Canaanites." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> In this passage there is an allusion and a hint concerning the evil inclination, for the evil inclination slanders against a person and causes him to sin constantly, and it comes to him in the likeness of a beautiful woman to mislead him and to ensnare him in all the desires of the world. And this is the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said: 'The Torah spoke only against the evil inclination' — they explained to us that the subject of this passage of the beautiful woman is a hint to the evil inclination. And what is a person's remedy so that he may be saved from its harm? That he should remove its hard power from himself — a complete removal. This is: וגלחה את ראשה — for a person's hair is his strength, in the manner of what is written (Judges 16:17): אם גלחתי וסר ממני כחי. And therefore Scripture used the expression of shaving, meaning that he should not follow it at all but rather remove it from himself entirely. And what more should he do? He should reflect upon the merit of the first man before he sinned, whose garment was a fingernail. This is: ועשתה את צפרניה — meaning that that great merit should be in his eyes. And with this he will remove the garment of captivity, which is beautiful garments — meaning, he will remove the physical desires and loathe them and take from them only what is permitted. This is: וישבה בביתך — meaning, you may keep them with you to make use of them. And how? By weakening its powers first. This is: ובכתה את אביה ואת אמה ירח ימים — all this time, which encompasses all the circuits and all the powers of idolatry and the evil inclination. And afterward: תבא אליה — and after that you may make use of it in everything you need, in the ways of the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:02.584382459Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}